WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 98.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.440
It's June 11th, 2026.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.700
Here's your space in science briefing.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.760
Web has now reached the era when the universe's very first stars

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.000
and galaxies formed.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.040
After four years of operations, the James Webb Space Telescope

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.220
has extended observations to the period

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.780
when the earliest stars and galaxies formed.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.380
UCL astronomer Richard Ellis and collaborators

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.880
are using the data to trace the initial buildup

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:28.980
of cosmic structure.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.480
The telescope's infrared sensitivity

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.500
allows it to detect light from objects

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.980
more than 13 billion years old.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.820
Follow-up programs are now prioritizing

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.880
the faintest, most distant sources.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.900
Webb's observations of glimpse 1777-75

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.800
show a black hole still embedded in a dense gas cocoon

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.560
1.8 billion years after the big bang.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.000
The surrounding material absorbs most x-rays,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.840
explaining why these little red dots appear faint

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:58.860
at high energies.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.920
Typical growing black holes lack such thick cocoons

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.940
and therefore shine brightly across multiple wavelengths.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.840
The finding adds one more data point

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.160
to the puzzle of how supermassive black holes

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.480
assembled so quickly in the early universe.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.820
Next, astronomers will search for similar objects

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.220
to test whether dense cocoons were common at that epoch.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.980
On a completely different note, Italian fashion house Prada

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.840
is now working directly with NASA

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.580
on the next generation of lunar spacesuits.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.500
The collaboration targets improved mobility

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.600
and thermal protection for extended surface operations.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.640
Engineers are integrating advanced materials

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.580
that must withstand extreme temperature swings on the moon.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.160
The suit will support crude landings

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.420
planned after the initial Artemis III mission.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.440
Europe is now outlining its own independent lunar surface

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.560
activities after that first landing.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.620
European space agency officials are planning

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.440
distinct contributions to exploration infrastructure

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.560
once Artemis III concludes.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.020
Discussions include science instruments

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.860
and potential logistics support for sustained presence.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.420
These efforts build on existing ESA participation

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.960
in the Artemis program.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.980
Next, the agency has awarded contracts

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.340
for a new generation of radar satellites.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.020
Thalouselina space and Airbus defense and space

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.520
received contracts to build next generation radar satellites

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:23.920
for the Copernicus program.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.360
The spacecraft will deliver higher resolution imaging

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.160
for environmental monitoring and disaster response.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.540
Both companies will incorporate advanced synthetic

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.360
aperture radar technology.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.420
The satellites are expected to extend Europe's

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.520
Earth observation capabilities through the 2030s.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.760
Shifting focus to launch vehicles,

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.380
Arian 6 is gaining upgraded solid boosters.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.960
The P160C solid motor replaces

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.140
the earlier P120C and carries 14 tons more propellant.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.660
The upgrade delivers roughly 10% more performance

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.860
to orbit for the same rocket configuration.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.520
Structural work occurs in Italy,

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.560
nozzles in France and igniters in Norway.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.420
The first flight with the new motors is scheduled

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.180
for an upcoming Arian 6 mission carrying Amazon satellites.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.360
On another continent, Australia is bringing

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.160
a historic radio telescope back into service.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.880
The historic Malanglo Observatory has been rescued

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.780
from retirement and restored for new observations.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.640
Engineers have upgraded receivers and signal processing

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.340
to support modern radio astronomy projects.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.100
The telescope's large collecting area

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.800
offers sensitivity for surveys of pulsars

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:39.960
and transient events.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.300
Australian researchers expect the revived facility

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.260
to contribute to international observing campaigns.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.200
If the sun were scaled to the size of a basketball

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.060
sitting in downtown Toronto,

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.000
the nearest star would still be another basketball

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.820
roughly 7,000 kilometers away in Vancouver.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.260
Star color works the same way a heated metal bar

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.160
changes hue.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.320
Hotter objects emit shorter wavelength light

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.980
that our eyes register as blue or white,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.260
while cooler ones peak at longer red wavelengths.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.180
A star only 3,000 Kelvin cooler than the sun

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.880
appears noticeably orange.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.480
One 10,000 Kelvin hotter looks blue white.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.020
The exact shade also shifts as the star ages

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.840
and its surface temperature changes by hundreds of degrees

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.200
over millions of years.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.220
We can therefore read a star's temperature

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.380
and roughly its mass and age from a single glance

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.260
on a clear night.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.420
Yet the precise mapping between observed color

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.280
and interior structure still contains gaps

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.220
for stars caught in rapid evolutionary phases.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.880
The current population of small satellites

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.860
has reached several thousand units in low Earth orbit.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.940
Most belong to large commercial constellations

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.520
providing communications or imaging services.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.540
Launch rates continue to climb as cost per kilogram fall.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.440
Operators are now studying how this density

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.300
affects collision risk and orbital debris management.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.180
Regulatory bodies are tracking the growth

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.340
to update guidelines on orbital slots

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.740
and end of life disposal.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.120
Keoxia is presenting radiation tolerant memory solutions

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.140
at HPE Discover Las Vegas, 2026.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.520
These storage systems target the demanding conditions

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.280
of AI workloads, hybrid cloud and deep space missions.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:29.400
Keoxia's technology must handle high radiation levels

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.540
while maintaining data integrity over long durations.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.440
The announcements focus on future hardware

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.200
for both commercial and government space programs.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.440
The company is also highlighting endurance testing

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.500
that simulates years of exposure in harsh environments.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.480
Experiments are also testing crop growth

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.920
under lunar and microgravity conditions

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.240
to support long duration missions.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.720
Researchers focus on closed loop systems

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.680
that recycle water and nutrients with minimal mass.

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.480
Early trials use hearty plants

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:04.020
that can tolerate radiation and limited light.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.480
Success would reduce reliance on resupply missions

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:08.660
for future crews.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.180
Additional tests are exploring nutrient delivery methods

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.260
that function without traditional soil

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.780
or gravity driven flow.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.640
Three geostationary satellites were recently canceled,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.260
reducing expected premiums for insurers.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.640
SES disclose the pullbacks after a period

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.480
of heavy claims from other losses.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.000
Legacy GE OSets had long provided steady income

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.320
that offset riskier segments of the market.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.800
The shortfall now pressures carriers

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.380
to adjust rates or seek new business lines.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.000
Analysts are watching whether underwriters

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.820
will shift focus toward low earth orbit coverage

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.740
to balance their portfolios.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.760
The British firm Open Cosmos has requested more time

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.320
to deploy its planned European sovereign broadband network.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.200
Launch delays have pushed the original schedule

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.160
beyond the current regulatory window.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.240
The constellation is intended

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.040
to provide independent connectivity for the continent.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.880
Regulators are reviewing the extension request

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.400
alongside updated deployment milestones.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.460
The revised timeline includes phased launches

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.020
that prioritize coverage over key European regions first.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.380
China's largest operational rocket placed

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.420
a classified payload into orbit from Winchang,

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.360
a separate commercial launch

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.680
of the Zhu Que IIE vehicle carried test satellites

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.400
for direct-to-device communications.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.880
Both missions advance national

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.900
and commercial communications infrastructure.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.160
The Zhu Que launches occurred within hours of each other.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.080
Ground teams confirmed successful insertion

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.860
into their respective target orbits

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.320
shortly after liftoff.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.300
A Falcon 9 is scheduled to lift 24 starling satellites

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:52.040
from Vandenberg Space Force Base at 805 AM Pacific daylight time.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:55.760
The mission will deploy the satellites into a polar orbit

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.380
to expand global coverage.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.880
Liftoff occurs one day before SpaceX begins

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.640
public trading on Nasdaq.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.840
The launch marks the company's continued high cadence

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.600
of constellation replenishment.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.540
Recovery of the first-stage boosters planned

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.720
for a drone ship stationed in the Pacific.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:18.160
Born in 1888, Chesley Bonestell created realistic paintings

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:19.940
of space that spanned decades.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.600
His best-known work came from the 1952 to 1954

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.860
Call Your Series Man Will Conquer Space Soon,

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:30.140
produced with Wernberg von Braun.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:33.460
Those illustrations helped turn speculative ideas

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.160
into believable engineering targets for the public.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.080
His art appeared in many magazines and books

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.620
through the mid-20th century.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:43.740
Later retrospectives have noted how his attention

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.180
to lighting and perspective influenced public expectations

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.720
for actual lunar and planetary imagery.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.400
Star color directly reflects surface temperature

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.740
with blue stars hottest and red stars coolest.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:00.140
Observers can estimate temperature by eye

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.640
once they learn the sequence from blue white

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.360
through yellow to orange red.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.480
The article explains how atmospheric conditions

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.660
and the star's evolutionary stage

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.900
also influence perceived hue.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:15.480
Simple backyard observations can therefore reveal

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.940
basic stellar properties without instruments.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.480
Seasonal changes in viewing angle can further alter

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:24.000
the apparent tint for stars near the horizon.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.100
Before we go, keep an eye on further updates

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.400
from the Copernicus Radar satellite program

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.640
as the new contracts move into production.

